sec.p:
To quantify the compensatory mechanisms of hyperexcitability with simulation-based inference, we implemented a phenomenological spiking neuronal network simulator (Figure 7). We used adaptive exponential leaky integrate-and-fire neurons (AdEx) (Brette & Gerstner, 2005) to model one excitatory and two inhibitory populations connected with conductance-based Tsodyks-Markram synapses to model short-term synaptic plasticity (Tsodyks et al., 1998). The voltage of the AdEx neuron changes according to Equation 1:
(1)
Cdvdt=(−gL(v−EL)+gLΔTexp⁡(v−VTΔT)−w+Itotal) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C \frac{d v}{d t}=\left(-g_{L}\left(v-E_{L}\right)+g_{L} \Delta T \exp \left(\frac{v-V_{T}}{\Delta T}\right)-w+I_{\text {total }}\right) \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
C is the membrane capacitance, v is the membrane voltage, t it the current time, gL is the leak conductance, EL is the leak conductance’s equilibrium potential, ΔT is the slope factor of the exponential term, VT is the exponential threshold, Itotal is the summed synaptic input (Equation 4), and w is the dynamic adaptation current:
(2)
τwdwdt=a(v−EL)−w \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{w} \frac{d w}{d t}=a\left(v-E_{L}\right)-w \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
τw is the adaptation time constant and a is the magnitude of the subthreshold adaptation. When the membrane voltage is larger than 0, v and w are reset according to Equation 3.1 and Equation 3.2, respectively:
(3.1)
Ifv>0:v→Vr \begin{document}$$\displaystyle v>0: v \rightarrow V_{r}$$\end{document}
(3.2)
w→w+b \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} w \rightarrow w+b \tag{3.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Vr is the reset voltage and b is the spike-triggered adaptation. All neuron model constants and parameters are in Table 1.

The conductances and the synaptic resources changed as in Equation 5.3, Equation 5.2, Equation 5.5, Equation 5.4 and Equation 5.1 (Tsodyks et al., 1998).
(5.1)
g=ASEy \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g=A_{\mathrm{SE}} y \tag{5.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(5.2)
dydt=−yτin \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d y}{d t}=\frac{-y}{\tau_{\mathrm{in}}} \tag{5.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(5.3)
dxdt=zτrec \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d x}{d t}=\frac{z}{\tau_{\mathrm{rec}}} \tag{5.3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(5.4)
z=1−x−y \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} z=1-x-y \tag{5.4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(5.5)
dudt=−uτfacil \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d u}{d t}=\frac{-u}{\tau_{\text {facil }}} \tag{5.5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
x, y, and z are fractions of synaptic resources. Resources in the recovered state (x) transition to the active state (y) when a presynaptic spike occurs (Equation 6.2). Resources in the active state (y) contribute to the synaptic conductance scaled by the synaptic weight ASE (Equation 5.1) and transition to the inactive state (z) with time constant τin (Equation 5.2). Resources transition to the recovered state (x) from the inactive state (z) with the time constant τrec (Equation 5.3). Since the synaptic resources are fractions, the inactive resources can be calculate given the other two resources (Equation 5.4). The facilitation value u decays with time constant τfacil, and it facilitates because it increases when a spike occurs (Equation 6.1), and it scales the transition to the active fraction (Equation 6.2 & Equation 6.3). The parameter USE determines the strength of the facilitation. If τfacil was set to 0, Equation 5.5 was deleted from the model, and u was kept constant at USE. Table 2 shows the synaptic parameters for all connections.
(6.1)
On presynaptic spike:u→u+USE(1−u) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle u \rightarrow u+U_{\mathrm{SE}}(1-u)$$\end{document}
(6.2)
y→y+ux \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} y \rightarrow y+u x \tag{6.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6.3)
x→x−ux \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x \rightarrow x-u x \tag{6.3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The model consists of three different populations: principal cells (PCs), adapting interneurons (AINs), and non-adapting interneurons (NINs) (Figure 7 A). Their parameters are largely taken from Naud et al. (2008). The AdEx model has nine parameters, and we additionally varied the number of neurons in the interneuron populations (see Table 1 for all parameters). We kept ΔT, τw, a, b, and Vr constant to keep the parameter space tractable for simulation-based inference (SBI) and to keep qualitative changes of firing patterns to a minimum. That left C, gL, EL and VT subject to SBI. This totals twelve free neuronal parameters plus the cell numbers of the two interneuron populations.

We introduced specific conditions on the posterior distribution to investigate the mechanisms that can compensate for interneuron loss, excitatory synaptic sprouting, and intrinsic hyperexcitability. To investigate interneuron loss we conditioned the number of AINs (AINn) and the number of NINs (NINn):
(7.1)
p(θ∣xBL,AINn=55,NINn=54) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{AIN}_{n}=55, \mathrm{NIN}_{n}=54\right) \tag{7.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(7.2)
p(θ∣xBL,AINn=15,NINn=15) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{AIN}_{n}=15, \mathrm{NIN}_{n}=15\right) \tag{7.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Equation 7.2 produces healthy dynamics (xBL), despite interneuron loss and Equation 7.1 is the healthy control, which produces healthy dynamics with a normal number of interneurons. θ are the unconditioned parameters. 55 and 54 are the MAP estimates for the interneuron counts of the marginal baseline posterior.

To investigate excitatory synaptic sprouting we conditioned the PC to PC connection probability (PC → PCP) and strength (PC→PCASE)\begin{document}$\left(\mathrm{PC} \rightarrow \mathrm{PC}_{A_{\mathrm{SE}}}\right)$\end{document}:
(8.1)
p(θ∣xBL,PC→PCP=0.15%,PC→PCASE=4.35nS) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{PC} \rightarrow \mathrm{PC}_{P}=0.15 \%, \mathrm{PC} \rightarrow \mathrm{PC}_{A_{\mathrm{SE}}}=4.35 \mathrm{nS}\right) \tag{8.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(8.2)
p(θ∣xBL,PC→PCP=3%,PC→PCASE=4.35nS) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{PC} \rightarrow P \mathrm{C}_{P}=3 \%, \mathrm{PC} \rightarrow \mathrm{PC}_{A_{\mathrm{SE}}}=4.35 \mathrm{nS}\right) \tag{8.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Equation 8.2 produces healthy dynamics despite excitatory sprouting. The connection probability 3% is double the baseline MAP estimate of 1.5%. For comparison we chose an order of magnitude smaller percentage points for Equation 8.1. The connection strength 4.35 nS is the MAP estimate of the marginal BL posterior.

To investigate intrinsic changes in principal cell excitability, we conditioned the PC leak conductance (PCgL\begin{document}$\mathrm{PC}_{g_{L}}$\end{document}) and PC equilibrium potential (PCEL)\begin{document}$\left(\mathrm{PC}_{E_{L}}\right)$\end{document}:
(9.1)
p(θ∣xBL,PCgL=3.0nS,PCEL=−68mV) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{PC}_{g_{L}}=3.0 \mathrm{nS}, \mathrm{PC}_{E_{L}}=-68 \mathrm{mV}\right) \tag{9.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(9.2)
p(θ∣xBL,PCgL=2.5nS,PCEL=−66mV) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{PC}_{g_{L}}=2.5 \mathrm{nS}, \mathrm{PC}_{E_{L}}=-66 \mathrm{mV}\right) \tag{9.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
These represent a minor increase of input resistance and resting membrane potential in principal cells. MAP estimate for baseline output of the two parameters was PCgL\begin{document}$\mathrm{PC}_{g_{L}}$\end{document} = 3.12 nS and PCEL=−67.87mV\begin{document}$\mathrm{PC}_{E_{L}}=-67.87 \mathrm{mV}$\end{document}.

To identify compensatory mechanisms of interneuron loss, we conditioned AINs (AINn) and the number of NINs (NINn) in the baseline (Equation 10.1) and hyperexcitable condition (Equation 10.2):
(10.1)
p(θ∣xBL,AINn=15,NINn=15) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{AIN}_{n}=15, \mathrm{NIN}_{n}=15\right) \tag{10.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(10.2)
p(θ∣xHE,AINn=15,NINn=15) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{HE}}, \mathrm{AIN}_{n}=15, \mathrm{NIN}_{n}=15\right) \tag{10.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For synaptic sprouting, we conditioned the PC to PC connection probability (PC → PCP) and strength (PC→PCASE)\begin{document}$\left(\mathrm{PC} \rightarrow \mathrm{PC}_{A_{\mathrm{SE}}}\right)$\end{document} in the baseline (Equation 11.1) and hyperexcitable (Equation 11.2) condition:
(11.1)
p(θ∣xBL,PC→PCP=3%,PC−PCASE=4.35nS) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{PC} \rightarrow P \mathrm{C}_{P}=3 \%, \mathrm{PC}-\mathrm{PC}_{A_{\mathrm{SE}}}=4.35 \mathrm{nS}\right) \tag{11.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(11.2)
p(θ∣xHE,PC→PCP=3%,PC→PCASE=4.35nS) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{HE}}, \mathrm{PC} \rightarrow \mathrm{PC}_{P}=3 \%, \mathrm{PC} \rightarrow \mathrm{PC}_{A_{\mathrm{SE}}}=4.35 \mathrm{nS}\right) \tag{11.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For depolarization, we conditioned the PC leak conductance (PCgL\begin{document}$\mathrm{PC}_{g_{L}}$\end{document}) and PC equilibrium potential (PCEL)\begin{document}$\left(\mathrm{PC}_{E_{L}}\right)$\end{document} in the baseline (Equation 12.1) and hyperexcitable (Equation 12.2) condition:
(12.1)
p(θ∣xBL,PCgL=2.5nS,PCEL=−66mV) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{BL}}, \mathrm{PC}_{g_{L}}=2.5 \mathrm{nS}, \mathrm{PC}_{E_{L}}=-66 \mathrm{mV}\right) \tag{12.1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12.2)
p(θ∣xHE,PCgL=2.5nS,PCEL=−66mV) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\theta \mid x_{\mathrm{HE}}, \mathrm{PC}_{g_{L}}=2.5 \mathrm{nS}, \mathrm{PC}_{E_{L}}=-66 \mathrm{mV}\right) \tag{12.2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
